For what ordered pair $(a,b)$ are there infinite solutions $(x,y)$ to the system \begin{align*}
2ax+2y&=b,\\
5x+y&=-3?
\end{align*}

response:
To solve the problem, we can set up a system of equations with the given conditions. The point Z is the intersection of the lines represented as below:
\begin{aligned}
5y &= 2x \\
3x - 4y &= 7
\end{aligned}
Cross multiply 5 on the LHS of the first and divide 2 on the RHS of the first, the system can be equivalently refined into 
\begin{aligned}
15y &= 6x \\
3x - 4y &= 7
\end{aligned}
Subtract the second equation from the first and cancel out x's, we'll arrive at:
\begin{aligned}
19y &= 7y + 7 \Rightarrow 12y = 7 \\
y &= \frac{7}{12}
\end{aligned}
Plug it in 4y into the second equation and simplify,
\begin{aligned}
3x - 4\left(\frac{7}{12}\right) &= 7 \\
3x - \frac{7}{3} &= 7 \\
3x &= 7 + \frac{7}{3} \\
x &= \frac{28}{9}
\end{aligned}
Since we know the two coordinates at Z are $\left(\frac{28}{9}, \frac{7}{12}\right)$, that means the the sum of the coordinates at $Z$ is 
$\left(\frac{28}{9}+\frac{7}{12}\right) = \frac{127}{36}$.
</think> <answer> $\frac{127}{36}$ </answer>

