What are some applications of calculus of variations in quantum and topological field theories?

EDIT:  I added a short definition at Calc. of Var. and PDE to make it self-contained. The minimization problems that you want to solve in these two points correspond to some different extremum problems (although, depending on one's view, they might be equally legitimate). 
In all these cases, we'll say that we want to minimize the action functional.
Of course, here you see how particles behave (either at the atomic or subatomic level) because that action functional is determined entirely by the potential and field equations, i.e. you can't extract anything meaningful about the particle's  structure whatsoever as a result of all the actions associated with the various forms of interaction.
I believe that this perspective fits with most of the learning in undergraduate physics since one often skips the mathematics as much as possible. In fact, the physical intuition I described above is very similar to the way Wigner perceived it in the 1930s.
More recently though, mathematicians have been using various variational principles to prove existence theorems for a wide range of generalized functions, C*-algebras, the Geometric Rigidity Theorem, general relativity, and a good number of other subjects. At one time I tried to write a review of his work, but ended up instead exploring some of his book "Measure Theory" which is awesome for its Jordan decomposition philosophy, in which one takes almost all the obvious things the book would like to mention and boils down to just a dozen or so little bits. His book used since Rings of Measures is excellent, too.

