response:
 This probability problem can be solved using combinations. Let's consider the number of ways we can select exactly two, three, or all four of the top three students out of the total group of 28 students.
Answer: <answer> The total number of ways to select any four students out of 28 is $\binom{28}{4}$. To find the probability of selecting at least two of the top three students, we calculate the sum of the ways to select exactly two, three, or all four of those students and divide by the total number of ways.
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Probability} &= \frac{\binom{3}{2}\binom{25}{2} + \binom{3}{3}\binom{25}{1} + \binom{3}{4}\binom{25}{0}}{\binom{28}{4}} \\
&= \frac{3 \times 300 + 1 \times 25 + 0 \times 1}{20475} \\
&= \frac{925}{20475} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{37}{819}}
\end{aligned}
\]

