A square has a side length of 10 inches. Congruent isosceles right triangles are cut off each corner so that the resulting octagon has equal side lengths. How many inches are in the length of one side of the octagon? Express your answer as a decimal to the nearest hundredth. [asy]
size(150);
draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(10,10)--(0,10)--(0,0),linewidth(0.7));
draw((5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2)),0)--(0,5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2))),linewidth(0.7));
draw((10 - (5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2))),0)--(10,5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2))),linewidth(0.7));
draw((0,(10-(5*sqrt(2)/(1+sqrt(2)))))--(5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2)),10),linewidth(0.7));
draw((10 - (5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2))),10)--(10,10 - 5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2))),linewidth(0.7));
[/asy]

response:
 Let $x$ be the side length of the octagon. [/think] <answer> $$5.57.$$  [/answer]

**Solution:**

Let the congruent isosceles right triangles to be cut off each corner have side length $x$. Hence, $x\sqrt{2}=10\rightarrow x=\frac{10}{\sqrt{2}}=\frac{10\sqrt{2}}{2}=5\sqrt{2}$ inches. Consider one of the corner triangles that is cut off. The octagon formed is as shown below. [asy]
usepackage("amsmath");
size(150);
draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(10,10)--(0,10)--(0,0),linewidth(0.7));
draw((5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2)],0)--(0,5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2)},linewidth(0.7));
draw((10 - (5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2)},0)--(10,5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2]),linewidth(0.7));
draw((0,10 - (5*sqrt(2)/(1+sqrt(2)})--(5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2}),10),linewidth(0.7));
draw((10 - (5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2}),10)--(10,10 - 5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2)},linewidth(0.7));
draw((10,10-alpha(x))--(10-x,10),linewidth(0.7));
draw((10,10),dot,linewidth(4));
draw((10 - (5*sqrt(2)/(1 + sqrt(2}),10)--(10,10 - 5),linewidth(0.7));
draw((10 - (5\sqrt{2}/(1 + \sqrt{2}),1.2)--(10,10 - 5),linewidth(0.7));
[/asy] Since the side lengths of the octagon are all of equal length, we know that the length of the hypotenuse and the other non-hypotenuse side is always equal to $5$ inches. Let's say that $x$ units is the side opposite the non-hypotenuse side. From the symmetry of the image and a little visual inspection, we see that the $x$ units are labeled only once, confirming that our claim that $x=5$ units indeed is correct; hence, $5.57$ is the length of one side of the octagon. $\blacksquare$

