The polynomial $p(x)$ has zeros $2009 + 9002\pi i$, $2009$, and $9002$. Since these are the only roots of $p(x)$, the polynomial must have degree at most three. A polynomial of degree $3$ in which one of the roots has nonzero imaginary part must have two distinct roots with nonzero imaginary parts. Therefore, $p(x)$ has exactly two distinct roots with nonzero imaginary parts and one root with zero imaginary part.<end think> <answer>



From Exercise 1.3.28(d) we have the following solution.

Assume that the polynomial $p(X) = X^3 + aX^2 + bX + c$ has three distinct zeros. In particular, these zeros are $r, s, t, u \in \Bbb C$ such that \begin{align*} X^3 + aX^2 + bX + c = (X - r)(X - s)(X - t)(X - u). \end{align*} Note that the constant term of $X^3 + aX^2 + bX + c$ is $-r s t u\,.$ Thus, since $(r - s)(r - t)(r - u) = s t u$, we must have either $r - s = 0$ or $t - u = 0$, which is a contradiction. Therefore, we're only allowed to have two roots $z$ and $\bar z$ with $z \ne \bar z$. The given information then allows us to conclude that $z = 2009+i9002$, and therefore $\bar z = 2009 - i9002$.

Thus, if $X^{12} + a X^8 + bX^4 + c = (X^4 - r^2X^2 - z^2)(X^4 - \bar r^2 X^2 - \bar z^2),\,$ where $r^2 = a/2$, then $c$ is negative and the other two roots are $\pm i\sqrt{r^2 - z^2}, \pm i \sqrt{\bar r^2 - \bar z^2}$.  Clearly the given $p(X)$ is
\begin{align*} X^{12} + \overline {(2009+i9002) (2009-i9002 )} X^8 - (2009+i9002) (2009-i9002 )X^4 + (2009+i9002) (2009-i9002 ) \end{align*}
\begin{align*} X^{12} + 2009\cdot 2009 + 9002 \cdot 9002 X^8 - (2009^2 + 9002^2) X^4 + (2009^2 + 9002^2) \end{align*}
\begin{align*} X^{12} + 4036 \cdot 2009 X^8 - 2009^2 - 9002^2 X^4 + 2009^2 + 9002^2 \end{align*}

The other $p(X)$ is:
\begin{align*} X^{12} + \overline {(2009+i9002) (2009-i9002 )} X^8 - (2009+i9002) (2009-i9002 )X^4 + (2009+i9002) (2009-i9002 ) \end{align*}

Thus, the answer is \begin{align*} \boxed{1} \end{align*}

