 My approach to this problem is to first draw the triangle $ABC$ with $\angle C = 90^\circ$ and then to draw a line from point $D$ to point $B,$ where $D$ is the midpoint of $\overline{AC}.$  This creates two right triangles, $\triangle BDC$ and $\triangle ADC.$  I am given that $\sin \angle DBC = \frac{3}{5},$ so I can set up the following equations:
\begin{align*}
\sin \angle DBC &= \frac{DB}{BC} \\
\sin \angle DBC &= \frac{\sqrt{13}}{5}.
\end{align*}I also know that $D$ is the midpoint of $\overline{AC},$ so I can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of $AC$: \begin{align*}
\overline{AC}^2 &= \overline{AB}^2 + \overline{BC}^2 \\\ не Cutter: \overline{AD} &= \frac{\overline{AC}}{2}.\end{align*}I also know that $\angle ABC = 90^\circ - \angle ABC,$ so I can use the identity $\tan(90^\circ - \theta) = \cot \theta$ to find $\tan \angle ABC$: \[\tan \angle ABC = \cot \angle ABC = \frac{\overline{AD}}{\overline{AB}}.\]Now, I can use the values of $\sin \angle DBC$ and $\overline{AD}$ to find $\tan \angle ABC$: \begin{align*}
\tan \angle ABC &= \frac{\overline{AD}}{\overline{AB}} \\\ne Cutter: \frac{\frac{\overline{AC}}{2}}{\overline{AB}} &= \frac{\sqrt{13}}{5}\cdot 2 \\\ne Cutter: \frac{\overline{AC}}{\overline{AB}} &= \frac{2\sqrt{13}}{5}.\end{align*}Therefore, \[\tan \angle ABC = \boxed{\frac{2\sqrt{13}}{5}}.\]</think> <answer>2sqrt13/5</answer>

